# Check that --emit-relocatable produces a relocatable object standing in for
# the object files the debug map names: the input's sections and symbols, plus
# the linked DWARF with a relocation for every machine address.

RUN: llvm-mc -triple x86_64-apple-darwin -filetype=obj \
RUN:   %p/../Inputs/relocatable-object.s -o %t.o
RUN: sed -e "s|@OBJ@|%/t.o|g" %p/../Inputs/relocatable-object.map > %t.map
RUN: dsymutil --linker classic --emit-relocatable -y %t.map -o %t.reloc.o

The output is an object file, not a dSYM companion.

RUN: llvm-objdump --macho --private-headers %t.reloc.o \
RUN:   | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=FILETYPE
FILETYPE:      magic {{.*}} filetype
FILETYPE-NEXT: MH_MAGIC_64 {{.*}} OBJECT

It carries the input's sections as well as the DWARF, so it can replace the
input in a link.

RUN: llvm-objdump --section-headers %t.reloc.o | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=SECTS
SECTS-DAG: __text
SECTS-DAG: __data
SECTS-DAG: __debug_info
SECTS-DAG: __debug_line
SECTS-DAG: __debug_abbrev

Addresses in .debug_info are relocated against the section they fall in, which
is how a compiler emits them, so the DWARF still reads correctly unrelocated.

RUN: llvm-objdump -r --section=__debug_info %t.reloc.o \
RUN:   | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=RELOCS
RELOCS:     RELOCATION RECORDS FOR [__debug_info]:
RELOCS-DAG: X86_64_RELOC_UNSIGNED    __text
RELOCS-DAG: X86_64_RELOC_UNSIGNED    __data

RUN: llvm-dwarfdump --verify %t.reloc.o | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=VERIFY
VERIFY: No errors.

RUN: llvm-dwarfdump --debug-info %t.reloc.o | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=DWARF
DWARF-DAG: DW_AT_low_pc{{.*}}0x0000000000000000
DWARF-DAG: DW_AT_low_pc{{.*}}0x0000000000000030
DWARF-DAG: DW_AT_location{{.*}}DW_OP_addr 0x40
DWARF-DAG: DW_AT_location{{.*}}DW_OP_addr 0x50

The debug map is not carried over: the DWARF is in the object now, so a later
consumer must not be sent looking for object files that need not exist.

RUN: llvm-nm -a %t.reloc.o | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=SYMS
SYMS-NOT: OSO
SYMS-DAG: _g_func
SYMS-DAG: _g_var

Replacing the input's own __DWARF renumbers the sections after it, so anything
still naming one by index has to be renumbered with them. llvm-readobj rejects
a stale index outright, and _trailing_sym sits past the DWARF to have one.

RUN: llvm-readobj --symbols %t.reloc.o | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=SECTIDX
SECTIDX: Name: _trailing_sym
SECTIDX: Section: __trailing
